    "title": "<p>Advantages and disadvantages of different cytopathological samples for FISH analysis</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "María D. Lozano",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 697.4pt; border-collapse: collapse; \" border=\"0\" width=\"930\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\" \">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.65pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"218\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; \">Type of cytopathological sample</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.35pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"363\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; \">Advantages</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 261.4pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"349\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; \">Disadvantages</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.65pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"218\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded cell block</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"363\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Preservation of tissue architecture</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">No overlapping cells</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cell blocks made from FNAB may be enriched in tumour cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 261.4pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"349\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Formalin fixation</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Probe signal loss due to nuclear truncation from sectioning</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Some cell blocks are poorly cellular</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">May have autofluorescent background that obscures signals</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.65pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"218\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; \">Smears</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"363\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">No nuclear truncation artefact</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Allows rapid onsite evaluation (ROSE) of specimen adequacy at time of procedure</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">No alterations related to formalin fixation</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Smears made from FNAB may be enriched in tumour cells</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 261.4pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"349\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Cell overlapping</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">May have autofluorescent background</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Legal issues due to loss of diagnostic smears</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.65pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"218\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Cytospins</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"363\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">No nuclear truncation artefact</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cells are concentrated in a smaller area</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 261.4pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"349\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">May have inadequate cellularity</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" \">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.65pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"218\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; \">Liquid-based cytopathology</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.35pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"363\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">No nuclear truncation artefact</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Monolayer preparation minimizes nuclear overlap, although tissue fragments tend to round up and become denser</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Less background</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Cells are concentrated in a smaller area</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 261.4pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"349\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">More expensive than other cytopathological preparations</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">Special instrumentation needed for slide preparation</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; \">May have inadequate cellularity</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  }
]